      "id": "ninh-binh-rainy-2026-11-15",
      "time": "20:00",
      "kind": "long",
      "caption": "# Ninh Binh on Rainy Days: What to Do When It Pours\n\nRain in Ninh Binh is usually a hard afternoon downpour, not a day-long washout. It rolls in around 2 or 3pm, lasts under an hour, and by dinner you would barely know it happened. So the move is simple: outdoor stuff in the morning, rain plan for the afternoon.\nThe boat still runs. Trang An and Tam Coc go out in light to moderate rain and only stop for a real typhoon. Trang An handles it better, the caves give you shelter for part of the route, and the limestone under grey sky looks like an ink painting. Go before 11am, ahead of the usual downpour window. A poncho beats an umbrella on a boat.\nBai Dinh Pagoda is the best dry backup. Covered corridors connect the halls, so you can see a lot without getting soaked, and the electric buggy cuts the walking between sections.\nHoa Lu Ancient Capital works too. The temples for King Dinh and King Le sit close together, so a guide can keep you moving between covered spots, and the old stone photographs well in flat grey light.\nA cooking class shifts indoors when the farm part cannot happen. Message the operator instead of assuming it is cancelled.\nSkip Mua Cave in heavy rain. Five hundred slippery steps are not worth it.\nFull guide, with the details: https://vietadvisor.travel/ninh-binh/ninh-binh-on-rainy-days",
      "photos": [
        "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vietadvisor-travel/vietadvisor-videos/claude/cowork-chat-reading-me8z1v/paste-helper/2026-11-15/ninh-binh-rainy/01.jpg",
        "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vietadvisor-travel/vietadvisor-videos/claude/cowork-chat-reading-me8z1v/paste-helper/2026-11-15/ninh-binh-rainy/02.jpg",
